## How to resolve

1. Open each conflicted file listed above.
2. Resolve every conflict-marker region thoughtfully. {% if replaying_commit_sha %}Preserve the replayed commit's intent on top of `{{ target }}` without anticipating later commits.{% else %}Preserve the intent of both merge heads.{% endif %}
3. Remove every `<<<<<<<`, `=======` and `>>>>>>>` marker line, so each file is
   left as valid, self-consistent content.
4. A listed file with NO conflict markers still conflicts -- git just could not
   write the two sides into it. It may be binary, a file on one side and a
   directory on the other, a file one side edited and the other deleted, or a
   path configured so git merges it whole. Open it and work out which case it
   is (`git` is denied to you, but the file, its neighbours and the rest of the
   tree are not), then leave the tree holding the outcome you judge right --
   keeping a side, writing a merged version, or deleting the path. Ralph Workflow
   stages whatever you leave. Because there are no markers, it cannot SEE which
   outcome you chose, so you must call the completion tool to say you decided: a
   round with such a file that ends without it is failed and handed to the next
   candidate.

## Hard rules

- DO NOT run any git command. Ralph Workflow stages the resolved files and
  {% if replaying_commit_sha %}continues the rebase{% else %}creates the merge commit{% endif %} itself. You only edit file contents. Git is
  denied to this session by the exec policy, so attempting it wastes the round.
- Do not modify ANY file that is not listed under "Conflicted files" above.
  Not a neighbouring module, not a test, not an import, not a formatting nit.
  If the resolved code needs a change elsewhere, leave it: that belongs to a
  later run, not to this one.
{% if replaying_commit_sha %}- Ralph Workflow re-reads the worktree after you return. An edit to ANY tracked
  path that is not listed above REJECTS the whole resolution and aborts the
  rebase, discarding your work on the listed files too.
{% endif %}- A leftover conflict marker in ANY listed file REJECTS the whole resolution
  and {% if replaying_commit_sha %}aborts the rebase{% else %}aborts the merge{% endif %}. Ralph Workflow re-scans every listed path
  textually after you return, so for a file that HAS markers your own report is
  never the evidence. For a file that has none there is nothing to re-scan --
  that is why those need the completion call, and why an untouched one is
  landed exactly as you left it.

## Completion contract

When every listed file is resolved and marker-free, call the
{{ DECLARE_COMPLETE_TOOL_REFERENCE }} MCP tool with a short
summary of what you resolved.

Exiting cleanly WITHOUT calling {{DECLARE_COMPLETE_TOOL_REFERENCE}} is treated as a FAILED
round, not as a resolution.
